'''David Carl Crowley''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|r|aʊ|l|i}}; born May 14, 1986) is an American politician who has served as the seventh [[List of Milwaukee County executives|executive of Milwaukee County]] since 2020. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he previously represented [[Wisconsin's 17th Assembly district]], including the west side of [[Milwaukee]], from 2017 to 2020.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://projects.jsonline.com/topics/election/2020/4/13/wisconsin-spring-election-results-april-7.html |title=Wisconsin spring election results |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=April 13, 2020 |access-date=April 13, 2020}}</ref><ref name="election">{{cite news |last=Bice |first=Daniel |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/04/14/milwaukee-elections-larson-concedes-crowley-county-executive/2990129001 |title=State Sen. Chris Larson concedes to Rep. David Crowley in the Milwaukee County exec's race |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=April 14, 2020 |access-date=April 15, 2020}}</ref><ref name="assemblydepart">{{cite press release |url=http://www.thewheelerreport.com/wheeler_docs/files/061820hintz.pdf |title=Rep. Hintz Statement on Rep. Crowley's Final Day in the State Assembly |date=June 18, 2020 |work=Office of Representative Gordon Hintz |location=[[Madison, Wisconsin]] |via=[[The Wheeler Report]] |access-date=June 30, 2020}}</ref> Crowley is the first African American to serve as Milwaukee County's top official and the youngest person elected to the office, having taken office in May 2020 at age 33.<ref name="MJSfirst">{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2020/05/04/david-crowleys-journey-to-become-milwaukee-county-executive/3056663001 |title='People felt like this was my path': David Crowley's journey to becoming the first African American elected as Milwaukee County executive |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |last=Glauber |first=Bill |date=May 4, 2020 |access-date=May 5, 2020 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="APLaunch">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Democratic Milwaukee County leader David Crowley joins the race for Wisconsin governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-crowley-evers-milwaukee-8710d7eb3ba1a50a004eaa935939333e |agency=[[Associated Press]] |date=September 9, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Crowley is the Democratic nominee for [[governor of Wisconsin]] in the [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2026 election]], winning the nomination over [[Francesca Hong]] by a narrow margin of 0.48% in an upset after suspending his campaign in July, then re-entering the race 24 days before the August&nbsp;11 primary.<ref name="politico">{{cite web|first=Meredith|last=Hill|url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/08/11/wisconsin-minnesota-south-carolina-connecticut-elections|title=David Crowley defeats democratic socialist Francesca Hong in Wisconsin governor primary|website=[[Politico]]|date=August 12, 2026|accessdate=August 12, 2026}}</ref><ref name="APPrimary">{{cite news |last=Yoon |first=Robert |title=AP Decision Notes: What to expect in Wisconsin's state primary |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-primary-governor-evers-hong-crowley-house-van-orden-berge-cooke-5590c78df1ba710de351a2a753441c84 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |date=August 10, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Crowley was born on May 14, 1986, in [[Milwaukee]], Wisconsin.<ref name="WILeg">{{cite web |title=Representative David Crowley |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2019/legislators/assembly/1811 |website=[[Wisconsin Legislature]] |access-date=February 25, 2026}}</ref> Crowley's parents struggled<!-- Did they really struggle about it or is this just a [[WP:Euphemism]]? --> with drug addiction during his childhood, and the family lost its home when he was 10 years old. His parents eventually divorced. Crowley was evicted from two more homes before graduating from high school.<ref name="wapo">{{Cite news|url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/09/13/this-democrats-comeback-saga-began-long-before-his-primary-upset/ |title= This candidate won a stunning upset. His life before that is even less likely. |date= September 13, 2026 |first= Karen |last= Tumulty |author-link= Karen Tumulty  |newspaper= [[The Washington Post]] |accessdate= September 17, 2026 }}</ref><ref name="MJSfirst"/><ref name="APLaunch"/><ref name="WUWM2021">{{cite news |last=Quirmbach |first=Chuck |title=Low-income people who are facing eviction in Milwaukee County gain more access to free legal help |url=https://www.wuwm.com/2021-10-15/low-income-people-who-are-facing-eviction-in-milwaukee-county-gain-more-access-to-free-legal-help |work=[[WUWM]] |date=October 15, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Crowley graduated from [[Bay View High School (Milwaukee)|Bay View High School]], and first attended the [[University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee]] in 2006, leaving during his first semester and returning intermittently in later years.<ref name="WPRDegree">{{cite news |last=Casey |first=Evan |title=After dropping out at 19, Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley will graduate from college this weekend |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/milwaukee-county-executive-david-crowley-will-graduate-college |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=December 13, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> Under the mentorship of Reggie and Sharlen Moore and their community organizing group Urban Underground, as well as serving as a [[Public Allies|Public Ally]] and working with individuals impacted by the justice system at the nonprofit [[Joseph Ellwanger|Project RETURN]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Milwaukee County Exec David Crowley's Rise in Politics, leading MKE & running for Gov |url=https://milwaukeeuncut.com/episodes/milwaukee-county-exec-david-crowleys-rise-in-politics-leading-mke-running-for-gov/ |access-date=12 August 2026 |work=Milwaukee Uncut |publisher=Story Mark Studios |date=5 August 2026}}</ref> Crowley became interested in issues of policing and local government.<ref name="wapo"/><ref name="MJSfirst"/>

Crowley resumed his undergraduate studies while serving as county executive and earned a bachelor's degree in community engagement and education from the University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee in December 2024, delivering the [[Commencement speech|commencement address]] at his graduation ceremony.<ref name="wapo"/><ref name="WPRDegree"/><ref name="UWMDegree">{{cite web |title=UWM to award more than 1,700 degrees at commencement Dec. 15 |url=https://uwm.edu/news/uwm-to-award-more-than-1700-degrees-at-commencement-dec-15/ |website=[[University of Wisconsin–Milwaukee]] |date=December 9, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="APLaunch"/>

==Early political career==
While attending college, Crowley began working in politics as a campaign organizer for {{nowrap|U.S. Senator}} [[Russ Feingold|Russ Feingold's]] 2010 re-election campaign. After the [[2010 United States Senate election in Wisconsin|election]], he took a job as a legislative aide to Milwaukee County supervisor [[Nikiya Harris Dodd]]. When Harris Dodd was elected to the [[Wisconsin Senate]] in 2012, Crowley suspended his education to work as her policy director at the [[Wisconsin State Capitol]] in [[Madison, Wisconsin]].<ref name="MJSfirst"/><ref name="WILeg"/>

Crowley made his first attempt at elected office in 2016, when he ran for a seat on the [[Milwaukee Common Council]]. He came in third in the February nonpartisan primary election and did not advance to the general election.<ref name="MKE2016">{{cite web |title=February 16, 2016 - Spring Primary |url=https://city.milwaukee.gov/ElectionResults/2016/February16 |publisher=City of Milwaukee Election Commission |date=February 19, 2016 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> Later that month, his employer, Nikiya Harris Dodd, announced she would not seek re-election in [[Wisconsin's 6th Senate district]]. The move prompted Assembly member [[LaTonya Johnson (politician)|La Tonya Johnson]] to declare for that office, creating an open race in the [[Wisconsin's 17th Assembly district|17th Assembly district]]. Crowley entered the race and won the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] primary with 56% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2016/08/09/barnes-taylor-square-off/88428860 |title=Taylor easily defeats Barnes in primary |date=August 9, 2016 |access-date=May 5, 2020 |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref><ref name="WEC2016Primary">{{cite report |url=https://elections.wi.gov/sites/elections.wi.gov/files/Statewide%20Summary%20Results.pdf |title=Canvass Results for 2016 Partisan Primary - 8/9/2016 |date=September 30, 2016 |publisher=[[Wisconsin Elections Commission]] |access-date=May 5, 2020 |page=25 |archive-date=March 24, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210324162323/https://elections.wi.gov/sites/elections.wi.gov/files/Statewide%20Summary%20Results.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> He was unopposed in the general election and entered office on January 3, 2017.<ref name="MJSfirst"/><ref name="WEC2016General">{{cite report |url=https://elections.wi.gov/sites/elections.wi.gov/files/Statewide%20Results%20All%20Offices%20%28post-Presidential%20recount%29.pdf |title=Canvass Results for 2016 General Election - 11/8/2016 |date=December 22, 2016 |publisher=[[Wisconsin Elections Commission]] |access-date=May 5, 2020 |page=12 |archive-date=March 7, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200307173552/https://elections.wi.gov/sites/elections.wi.gov/files/Statewide%20Results%20All%20Offices%20%28post-Presidential%20recount%29.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> He was re-elected in 2018.<ref name="WILeg"/>

==Milwaukee County executive (2020–present)==
[[File:P20220905AS-000002 (52516571827).jpg|thumb|Crowley (furthest at left) greeting President [[Joe Biden]] with Milwaukee mayor [[Cavalier Johnson]] and others in 2022]]
In November 2019, after incumbent [[Chris Abele]] announced he would not seek re-election, Crowley announced his candidacy for [[Milwaukee County]] [[List of Milwaukee County executives|executive]].<ref name="announce2019">{{cite news |title=State Rep. David Crowley announces candidacy for Milwaukee County executive |url=https://www.fox6now.com/news/state-rep-david-crowley-announces-candidacy-for-milwaukee-county-executive |work=FOX6 News Milwaukee |date=November 4, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2019/10/16/milwaukee-county-executive-chris-abele-not-seeking-reelection/4001936002 |title=In a reversal, Milwaukee County Executive Chris Abele announces he will not seek re-election |date=October 16, 2019 |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |access-date=May 5, 2020 |last1=Spicuzza |first1=Mary |last2=Dirr |first2=Alison}}</ref> In the February primary election, Crowley came in a close second to state senator [[Chris Larson]], defeating county board chairman Theodore Lipscomb and businesswoman Purnima Nath.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/02/18/chris-larson-david-crowley-purnima-nath-theodore-lipscomb-sr/4790289002 |title=Chris Larson, David Crowley advance to April 7 election in Milwaukee County executive race |last1=Glauber |first1=Bill |last2=Dirr |first2=Alison |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=February 18, 2020 |access-date=May 5, 2020}}</ref> Crowley received the endorsement of outgoing executive Chris Abele, who had been challenged by Larson for the office in 2016. Abele went on to spend heavily on behalf of Crowley through his [[political action committee]], Leadership MKE.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/investigations/daniel-bice/2020/03/31/chris-abele-funded-group-has-spent-755-000-help-candidates/5093776002 |title=Bice: Chris Abele-funded group has spent staggering $755,000 for David Crowley and other preferred candidates |date=March 31, 2020 |last=Bice |first=Daniel |access-date=May 5, 2020 |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref><ref name="WPR2020">{{cite news |title=Rep. David Crowley emerges as next Milwaukee County executive |url=https://www.wpr.org/health/rep-david-crowley-emerges-next-milwaukee-county-executive |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 14, 2020 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> The April election was significantly disrupted by the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Wisconsin]]. Crowley defeated Larson by 1,026 votes.<ref name="election"/><ref name="WPR2020"/>

Crowley was sworn in as county executive on May 4, 2020, in the front yard of his home because of restrictions on public gatherings during the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/local/milwaukee/2020/05/04/david-crowley-sworn-next-milwaukee-county-executive/3077157001/ |title=David Crowley sworn in as next Milwaukee County executive |date=May 4, 2020 |access-date=May 5, 2020 |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |last=Dirr |first=Alison}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=David Crowley Sworn in as Milwaukee County Executive |url=https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/County-Executive/News/Press-Releases/David-Crowley-Sworn-in-as-Milwaukee-County-Executive |publisher=Milwaukee County |date=May 4, 2020 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> He resigned his seat in the Assembly effective June 18, 2020, as required by a 2015 Wisconsin law prohibiting a person from simultaneously serving as a member of the legislature and a county executive.<ref name="assemblydepart"/><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/local/milwaukee/2020/05/05/county-executive-david-crowley-resign-seat-state-assembly/3080240001/ |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley to resign seat in state Assembly |last=Dirr |first=Alison |date=May 5, 2020 |access-date=May 6, 2020 |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Wisconsin Statute 13.093(1) |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/document/statutes/13.093%281%29 |website=[[Wisconsin Legislature]] |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

As county executive, Crowley oversees an annual county budget of about $1.4 billion.<ref name="WPRBudget">{{cite news |last=Conquest-Ware |first=Steph |title=Milwaukee County supervisors adopt $1.4B budget but changes to transit routes, paratransit services unknown |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/milwaukee-county-supervisors-budget-transit-routes-paratransit-services |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=November 7, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Office of Strategy, Budget and Performance |url=https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/Strategy-Budget-and-Performance/Budget-Office |publisher=Milwaukee County |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> When he took office, the county was already facing longstanding financial problems that were compounded by the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref name="OpoienBeck2025">{{cite news |last1=Opoien |first1=Jessie |last2=Beck |first2=Molly |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley enters Democratic field for Wisconsin governor |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=September 9, 2025 |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/09/david-crowley-enters-democratic-field-for-wisconsin-governor/86039362007 |access-date=March 19, 2026}}</ref><ref name="WPRSalesTax">{{cite news |last=Casey |first=Evan |title=Milwaukee County Board passes 0.4 percent sales tax hike |url=https://www.wpr.org/economy/milwaukee-county-board-passes-sales-tax-hike |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 27, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> To address these problems, Crowley worked alongside organizations including the [[League of Wisconsin Municipalities]], the Greater Milwaukee Committee, and the Wisconsin Counties Association to seek additional local revenue authority.<ref name="Murphy2023">{{cite news |last=Murphy |first=Bruce |title=Murphy's Law: How David Crowley Led on Sales Tax |url=https://urbanmilwaukee.com/2023/08/23/murphys-law-how-david-crowley-led-on-sales-tax/ |work=[[Urban Milwaukee]] |date=August 23, 2023 |access-date=August 13, 2026}}</ref> He also lobbied Milwaukee mayor [[Cavalier Johnson]], Democratic and Republican state legislators, and Governor [[Tony Evers]] in support of a state funding agreement.<ref name="WPRSalesTax"/><ref name="Act12">{{cite web |title=2023 Wisconsin Act 12 |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2023/related/acts/12 |website=[[Wisconsin Legislature]] |date=June 20, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> The resulting legislation, 2023 Wisconsin Act 12, authorized Milwaukee County to impose an additional 0.4-percentage-point sales tax.<ref name="Act12"/><ref name="WPRSalesTax"/> The County Board approved the increase in July 2023, raising the county sales tax from 0.5% to 0.9%, and Crowley signed it the following month. The tax took effect on January 1, 2024.<ref name="WPRSalesTax"/><ref name="Gooden2023">{{cite news |last=Gooden |first=Isley |title=Milwaukee County sales tax increase, Crowley signs into law |work=FOX6 News Milwaukee |date=August 3, 2023 |url=https://www.fox6now.com/news/milwaukee-county-sales-tax-increase-signing |access-date=March 19, 2026}}</ref> County officials said the additional revenue was needed to address pension obligations and avoid further cuts to county services.<ref name="WPRSalesTax"/>

Crowley's other policies have addressed opioid addiction, housing affordability, and carbon emissions.<ref name="OpoienBeck2025"/><ref name="WPRDegree"/> In 2021, he signed legislation creating Right to Counsel Milwaukee, a program providing free legal representation to eligible residents facing eviction or foreclosure.<ref name="RightCounsel">{{cite press release |title=County Executive David Crowley Signs "Right to Counsel" into Law |url=https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/County-Executive/News/Press-Releases/County-Executive-David-Crowley-Signs-Right-to-Counsel-into-Law |publisher=Milwaukee County |date=July 27, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="WUWM2021"/> In 2023, Crowley signed legislation distributing more than $11 million in opioid-settlement funds among 15 county programs, including treatment for people in the county correctional system and vending machines dispensing [[naloxone]] and fentanyl test strips.<ref name="WPROpioids">{{cite news |last=Casey |first=Evan |title=Milwaukee County will place vending machines filled with free Narcan, fentanyl test strips throughout community |url=https://www.wpr.org/health/milwaukee-county-vending-machines-free-narcan-fentanyl-test-strips-opioid-overdose |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=March 13, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In April 2025, he signed Milwaukee County's Climate Action 2050 plan, which calls for net-zero carbon emissions from county operations by 2050.<ref name="ClimatePlan">{{cite press release |title=During Earth Month, County Executive David Crowley Signs Legislation Approving Climate Action 2050 Plan |url=https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/County-Executive/News/Press-Releases/During-Earth-Month-County-Executive-David-Crowley-Signs-Legislation-Approving-Climate-Action-2050-Plan |publisher=Milwaukee County |date=April 30, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Milwaukee County re-affirms Paris Climate Commitments |url=https://wisconsinexaminer.com/2026/04/02/milwaukee-county-re-affirms-paris-climate-commitments/ |work=Wisconsin Examiner |date=April 2, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref> 

In January 2021, Crowley was named to the ''Milwaukee Business Journal''{{'}}s "40 Under 40" class of 2021.<ref>{{cite news |date=January 12, 2021 |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley among Milwaukee Business Journal's 2021 "40 Under 40" class |url=https://wtmj.com/news/2021/01/12/milwaukee-county-executive-david-crowley-among-milwaukee-business-journals-2021-40-under-40-class/ |access-date=April 14, 2021 |work=WTMJ}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=40 Under 40: David Crowley, Milwaukee County Executive |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/milwaukee/news/2021/03/22/40-under-40-david-crowley.html |work=Milwaukee Business Journal |date=March 22, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> He was re-elected in 2024, defeating [[perennial candidate]] Ieshuh Griffin with 84.5% of the vote.<ref name="CountyBio">{{cite web |title=About David |url=https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/County-Executive/About-David |publisher=Milwaukee County |access-date=August 11, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260713221538/https://county.milwaukee.gov/EN/County-Executive/About-David |archive-date=2026-07-13 |url-status=live |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gatzow |first=Jessica |date=2024-04-03 |title=Milwaukee County election results: Exec. Crowley and Mayor Johnson win second terms |url=https://wtmj.com/news/2024/04/02/milwaukee-county-election-results-david-crowley-and-mayor-johnson-win-second-terms/ |access-date=2026-08-14 |website=WTMJ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240417035236/https://wtmj.com/news/2024/04/02/milwaukee-county-election-results-david-crowley-and-mayor-johnson-win-second-terms/ |archive-date=2024-04-17 |url-status=live |language=en-US}}</ref>

==2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial campaign==
{{main|2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election}}
In 2024, the Wisconsin political press began to speculate about whether Governor [[Tony Evers]] would run for a third term in the [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2026 gubernatorial election]]; at that time, Crowley was named as one possible candidate to succeed him.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://isthmus.com/news/cover-story/who-might-run-for-wisconsin-governor-in-2026 |title=Who might run for Wisconsin governor in 2026? |date=August 1, 2024 |first=Steven |last=Walters |work=[[Isthmus (newspaper)|Isthmus]] |access-date=February 14, 2025}}</ref> Evers announced on July 24, 2025, that he would not seek re-election.<ref name="APEvers">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin's Democratic governor, Tony Evers, won't seek reelection |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-gov-tony-evers-reelection-78b32ffc51dff53512fd7499f21e9878 |work=Associated Press |date=July 24, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> Crowley announced the next day that he planned to run for governor.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kremer |first=Rich |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley planning to enter race for Wisconsin governor |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/milwaukee-county-executive-david-crowley-planning-enter-race-wisconsin-governor |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 25, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Crowley formally launched his campaign for governor on September 9, 2025.<ref name="APLaunch"/><ref name="OpoienBeck2025"/> He was endorsed the next day by Milwaukee mayor [[Cavalier Johnson]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/10/milwaukee-mayor-cavalier-johnson-endorses-david-crowley-for-governor/86076612007 |title=Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson endorses David Crowley for governor, ending speculation about his own run |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |first=Molly |last=Beck |date=September 10, 2025 |access-date=September 10, 2025}}</ref> Crowley faced a crowded Democratic primary field that included Lieutenant Governor [[Sara Rodriguez]], former lieutenant governor [[Mandela Barnes]], state senator [[Kelda Roys]], state representative [[Francesca Hong]], former [[Wisconsin Department of Administration]] secretary [[Joel Brennan]], and [[Missy Hughes]], the former CEO of the [[Wisconsin Economic Development Corporation]].<ref name="WPRFundraising">{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title=Early fundraising numbers suggest competitive Democratic primary for governor |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/early-fundraising-numbers-competitive-democratic-primary-wisconsin-governor |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=January 16, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In the first major campaign finance reports&mdash;covering the second half of 2025&mdash;Crowley reported raising about $789,000 and had about $602,000 cash on hand, putting him in the strongest financial position of any of the gubernatorial candidates at that point.<ref name="WPRFundraising"/>

Despite his fundraising, Crowley struggled to gain support in opinion polls. A March 2026 Marquette University Law School poll found him at 3% among Democratic primary voters.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kremer |first=Rich |title=Poll: Chris Taylor leads Maria Lazar in Wisconsin Supreme Court race |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/poll-chris-taylor-leads-maria-lazar-wisconsin-supreme-court-race |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=March 25, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> At the [[Democratic Party of Wisconsin]] state convention in June, Crowley placed fourth in an informal straw poll, with 13% support from delegates.<ref name="MJS-strawpoll-2026">{{cite news |last=Beck |first=Molly |title=Sara Rodriguez leads field for governor in Wisconsin Democrats' straw poll |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/06/14/sara-rodriguez-leads-field-for-governor-in-wisconsin-dems-straw-poll/90529831007 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=June 14, 2026 |access-date=June 22, 2026}}</ref> On June 22, Hughes suspended her campaign and endorsed Rodriguez, who had won the straw poll.<ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title=Democrat Missy Hughes suspends campaign for governor, endorses Sara Rodriguez |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/democrat-missy-hughes-suspends-campaign-for-governor |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=June 22, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> Crowley announced on July 8 that he was suspending his campaign and formally endorsed Rodriguez the following day.<ref name="CrowleyExit">{{cite news |last=van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley ends bid for governor, endorses Sara Rodriguez |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/david-crowley-milwaukee-county-executive-governors-race |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 8, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/07/09/david-crowley-calls-on-democrats-to-clear-the-field-for-sara-rodriguez/90861208007 |title=David Crowley calls on Democrats to clear the field for Sara Rodriguez |first1=Molly |last1=Beck |first2=Zachary |last2=Suri |newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=July 9, 2026 |access-date=July 18, 2026}}</ref>

Days later, Rodriguez fired her campaign manager after discovering that her campaign had hundreds of thousands of dollars less cash on hand than expected. Rodriguez suspended her campaign on July 17, saying continuing problems with its financial reports would be a distraction.<ref name="APRodriguez">{{cite news |last=Beaumont |first=Thomas |title=David Crowley restarts Wisconsin governor bid after Sara Rodriguez quits race over finance problems |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-democratic-primary-rodriguez-money-problems-14a2cfc7fbae392a1016b94cb1c6d6fc |work=Associated Press |date=July 17, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>  As the Rodriguez campaign was collapsing, the ''[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]'' reported that the incumbent governor, Tony Evers, was "very seriously considering" a plan to bring Crowley back into the race.<ref>{{Cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/07/17/tony-evers-considering-move-to-draw-crowley-back-into-governors-race/90955480007/  |title= Evers considering move to draw David Crowley back into governor's race |first= Jessie |last= Opoien |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= July 17, 2026 |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref>  Hughes, who had previously dropped out and endorsed Rodriguez, tweeted "@DavidCCrowley get back in this race!"<ref>{{cite tweet|last= Hughes |first= Missy |user= MissyforWI |number= 2078147656493212071 |language= en-us |date= July 17, 2026 |accessdate= August 12, 2026 |title= .@DavidCCrowley get back in this race! }}</ref>  Crowley re-entered the race on July 18; Evers endorsed Crowley the next day.<ref name="APReturn">{{cite news |last=Kinnard |first=Meg |title=David Crowley reenters battleground Wisconsin's Democratic governor primary with Gov. Evers' backing |url=https://apnews.com/article/david-crowley-tony-evers-wisconsin-governor-a4ccb9e143e886e337d1f3b457b18207 |work=Associated Press |date=July 18, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="WPRReturn">{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Shawn |last2=Kremer |first2=Rich |title=Crowley returns to governor's race in last-minute effort to win Democratic primary |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/david-crowley-returns-governors-race-tony-evers-democratic-primary-2026 |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 18, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> A Marquette poll conducted from July 22 to 27 found Crowley at 7%, with 34% of Democratic primary voters undecided.<ref name="MarquetteJuly">{{cite web |title=Marquette Law Poll finds expanded support for Hong in Democratic primary; Barnes sits second and Crowley third |url=https://law.marquette.edu/poll/2026/07/29/marquette-law-poll-finds-expanded-support-for-hong-in-democratic-primary-barnes-sits-second-and-crowley-third/ |website=Marquette Law School Poll |date=July 29, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> 

Barnes suspended his campaign on July&nbsp;29,<ref>{{cite news |title=Mandela Barnes drops out of the Democratic primary for Wisconsin governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-barnes-democrats-primary-8d5c0f75dcc364b80fa41c7ab7b32407 |work=Associated Press |date=July 30, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> leaving Crowley, Hong, Brennan, and Roys as the only active candidates by the time of the August 11 primary.<ref name="APPrimary"/>  Taking to the campaign trail with Evers, Crowley blitzed across the state in the final two weeks before the primary;<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/08/03/tony-evers-hits-campaign-trail-with-david-crowley-to-stave-off-hong/91160104007/ |title= Tony Evers hits campaign trail for David Crowley to stave off Hong |first= Molly |last= Beck  |newspaper=  [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= August 3, 2026 |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref> his campaign also reported a surge in fundraising and a rush of new union endorsements.<ref>{{cite press release|url= https://www.wispolitics.com/2026/crowley-campaign-crowley-fundraising-surges-to-over-775000-raised-since-re-entering-race/  |title=  Crowley campaign: Crowley fundraising surges to over $775,000 raised since re-entering race |date= July 28, 2026 |work= Crowley for Wisconsin |via=[[Wispolitics.com]] |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/08/04/crowley-gets-union-endorsements-in-effort-to-boost-governor-campaign/91164293007 |title= Crowley gets union endorsements in effort to boost governor campaign |first= Ricardo |last= Torres |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= August 4, 2026 |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref>

Shortly after 3:00 a.m. on August 12, Crowley was declared the winner of the Democratic primary in what was described by journalists and analysts as a "stunning comeback" and a "big upset".<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/08/12/david-crowley-pulls-off-stunning-comeback-victory-over-francesca-hong/91156642007/ |title= David Crowley pulls off stunning comeback victory over Francesca Hong |first1= Molly |last1= Beck |first2= Hope |last2= Karnopp |first3= Mary |last3= Spicuzza |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= August 12, 2026  |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/08/12/how-david-crowley-upset-francesca-hong-wisconsins-primary-governor/ |title=How mainstream Democrats pulled off a big upset against a democratic socialist |date= August 12, 2026 |work= [[Washington Post]] |first1= Patrick |last1= Marley |first2= Teo |last2= Armus |first3= Clara |last3= Ence Morse |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref>  Crowley prevailed by about 3,700 votes over Francesca Hong, receiving about 39.8% of the statewide vote.  Evers congratulated Crowley in the early hours of August&nbsp;12, calling Crowley "the future of our party."<ref>{{Cite news|url= https://captimes.com/news/elections/david-crowley-upsets-hong-in-democratic-primary-for-wisconsin-governor/article_5c10d77c-c592-4188-89c4-fcf6a45da3a9.html |title=  Crowley wins upset, Hong concedes in Democratic primary for Wisconsin governor  |first= Olivia |last= Herken |date= August 12, 2026 |work= [[The Capital Times]] |accessdate= August 12, 2026 }}</ref> Crowley's win made him the first black man to be any major party's nominee for Wisconsin governor.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bentley |first1=Drake |title=David Crowley becomes first Black man to be major party's nominee for Wisconsin governor |url=https://milwaukeecourier.com/news/2026/08/12/david-crowley-becomes-first-black-man-to-be-major-partys-nominee-for-wisconsin-governor |access-date=31 August 2026 |work=The Milwaukee Courier |date=12 August 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

In the general election, Crowley will face Republican U.S. Representative [[Tom Tiffany]].<ref name="politico"/> If elected, Crowley would become Wisconsin's first African-American governor.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/08/12/david-crowleys-nomination-a-first-for-a-wisconsin-black-man/91270372007/| title=If elected, David Crowley would be Wisconsin's first Black governor}}</ref>

==Personal life and family==
Crowley married his wife, Ericka, in 2016; they have two daughters together, and Crowley is also the stepfather of a daughter from Ericka's previous relationship.<ref name="MJSfirst" /><ref name="CountyBio" /> Crowley has served as vice chair of the Milwaukee chapter of the [[American Civil Liberties Union]].{{citation needed|date=August 2026}} He has also served on the chapter's board.<ref name="WPRACLU">{{cite news |last=van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |title=Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley says he will not seek Senate seat |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/milwaukee-county-executive-david-crowley-says-he-will-not-seek-senate-seat |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 9, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}{{Dead link|date=August 2026 |fix-attempted=yes |url=}}</ref> He is a member of Milwaukee Urban League Young Professionals and the Milwaukee [[NAACP]].<ref name="CountyBio" /><ref name="announce2019" />

Crowley's parents struggled<!-- Did they really struggle about it or is this just a [[WP:Euphemism]]? If the latter, this should just say they were addicted to drugs. --> with drug addiction.<ref name="MJSfirst"/> His mother, Valerie, died in 2023.<ref name="WPRDegree"/>

